    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_ccefed678798dd23ef5549604c5aa313936963260854e683fe0c8dbe084c29ea",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "For negative time, the lack of a usable near-axis Dirichlet or logarithmic-derivative series forces this proof to obtain accurate H_t control by saddle analysis at imaginary depth comparable to log x; first-term dominance and a holomorphic derivative upgrade then make those estimates usable for the argument principle. The resulting negative-time zero count and classical-location bounds lose one logarithm relative to t=0, leaving control only down to fluctuations of logarithmic spatial size, while the inverse-distance zero ODE predicts that exactly this scale can relax during the available O(1) time before t=0. Thus the analytic representation bottleneck fixes a spatial resolution that is only barely compatible with the proposed dynamical route to local equilibrium.",
